## Why This Is Asked
Tests designing a mutable priority structure with time-based expiry and top-k queries, including updates and duplicates, under realistic constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Lazy expiry in priority queues to avoid frequent rebalancing
- O(log n) updates via delete+insert using an id map
- Tie-breaking with a unique id sequence to handle duplicates
- Top-k retrieval by temporarily extracting and reinserting items

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(id, score, expiry, seq) {
    this.id = id; this.score = score; this.expiry = expiry; this.seq = seq;
  }
}
class DynamicPriorityQueue {
  constructor() {
    this.treap = new Treap();
    this.idMap = new Map();
    this.seqCounter = 0;
  }

  insertItem(id, score, expiry) {
    this.removeItem(id);
    const node = new Node(id, score, expiry, ++this.seqCounter);
    this.treap.insert(node);
    this.idMap.set(id, node);
  }

  updateScore(id, newScore) {
    const node = this.idMap.get(id);
    if (node) {
      this.insertItem(id, newScore, node.expiry);
    }
  }

  removeItem(id) {
    const node = this.idMap.get(id);
    if (node) {
      this.treap.remove(node);
      this.idMap.delete(id);
    }
  }

  queryTopK(now, k) {
    const result = [];
    const iterator = this.treap.inOrder();
    for (const node of iterator) {
      if (result.length >= k) break;
      if (node.expiry > now) {
        result.push({id: node.id, score: node.score});
      }
    }
    return result;
  }
}
```

## Memory Trade-offs
- Additional O(n) space for id map and sequence counters
- Expired items remain in memory until explicitly removed or overwritten
- Lazy expiry reduces computational overhead at the cost of memory usage

## Why This Is Asked
This question simulates a live order book and tests dynamic range sums alongside top-k queries within a price band. It combines segment trees with per-node order statistics and requires careful handling of cancellations to maintain consistent state.

## Key Concepts
- **Dynamic segment tree** over the price domain for efficient range queries
- **Per-node multisets** to support kthLargest queries with duplicate prices
- **Order ID mapping** (idMap) for O(1) updates and cancellations
- **Amortized O(log² n)** operations: O(log n) tree traversal × O(log n) multiset operations

## Memory Handling
The idMap stores each active order's metadata, enabling O(1) lookups for updates and cancellations. When an order is cancelled, its entry is removed from both the idMap and the relevant multiset, ensuring no memory leaks. The segment tree nodes are created lazily on demand, only when prices are actually inserted.

## Cancellations
Cancellations require removing a specific quantity from a price node's multiset—O(log n) for the multiset operation plus O(log n) for updating the segment tree. This maintains accurate totals for both sum and kthLargest queries.

